  "_covers": "#1799 — WHAT THIS COUNT IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT. Every entry here was found by the variable's LITERAL NAME appearing in the file. A helper that writes names its CALLER supplies (`for (const [k,v] of Object.entries(vars)) env[k] = v`) has no literal name at the write, so the sweep cannot see it. Treat this number as a floor over the literal-name shape, never as the size of the class.",
  "_covers_measured": "Measured at 1ed4ab0f, and the gap is theoretical rather than open: 239 computed-key env writes across 84 files, of which 110 are deletes and 12 restore a saved snapshot. 19 are the helper shape, across 17 files. 12 of those files are already tracked here via their call sites, which DO name literally. The remaining 5 are quiet because they already route through the alias helpers (currentName/spellingsOf) or name no aliased variable at all. NO live instance was found. That is why this is a note and not a guard: a wider pattern cannot help, because the same syntax is benign in a restore loop and defective in a pinning one."
